  "result": "I revised `LVMC_11_52_030_B` without withdrawing it and wrote the new record to `record.jsonl`. Nothing has checked it yet; the harness validates it next and the coder rewrites the witnesses.\n\n**Why the gate refused it:** in the run's reading, neither \"commercial vehicle\" (`DEF-LVMC-11-02-070`) nor \"taxicab\" (`DEF-LVMC-11-02-140`) counts any road-user class as a member. So a vehicle's membership in either is never true. A commercial van or a taxicab halting within the sign's conditions therefore gets `unknown`, not `not_applicable`. That is how `ru.aggregate` is meant to behave. The node's mapping is not at fault, and I can't change the reading from here.\n\n**What changed:**\n- **Exception scenarios:** I removed the two `not_applicable` scenarios. No faithful rule can reach them under this reading, and a `branch_*` scenario can't expect `unknown`. In their place are two `violated` scenarios where the exceptions are decided false whatever the membership:\n  - `branch_1`: a commercial van halts in the yellow zone on a Sunday, outside the sign's hours.\n  - `branch_2`: a taxicab halts in the yellow zone under a sign whose vehicle class is commercial motor vehicle, so the sign's conditions don't hold for it.\n- **Mapping kept:** both groups stay as they are, never swapped for the narrower `ru.commercial_motor_vehicle` or `ru.taxicab` classes. The justifications and applicability text now say that each exception reads `unknown` when the sign's conditions hold but membership is unknown.\n- **Small fixes:** the `s3` narrowing now has `kind: \"reading\"`. I dropped the proposal for `p.halt_speed_tolerance`, which is already in the parameter catalogue.\n\n**Consequence for you:** while the reading leaves both groups without members, this rule can never excuse a commercial vehicle or taxicab. Those vehicles halting legitimately in a yellow zone will come out `unknown`. Only fixing the reading's group membership changes that. The coder suggested `ru.taxicab` for taxicabs and `ru.commercial_motor_vehicle` or `ru.truck` for commercial vehicles.",
